## Proof

Let $f$ be algebraic, so there exists a nonzero polynomial $P \in \mathbb{R}[x,y]$ such that
$$P(x, f(x)) = 0$$
identically on the domain of $f$.

Let $g$ be the inverse of $f$ on some open subset, so $f(g(y)) = y$ holds identically on the domain of $g$.

**Substitution.** Since $P(x, f(x)) = 0$ holds for all $x$ in the domain of $f$, and $g(y)$ lies in the domain of $f$ (because $f(g(y)) = y$ is well-defined), we may substitute $x = g(y)$:
$$P\bigl(g(y),\, f(g(y))\bigr) = 0.$$
Using $f(g(y)) = y$, this becomes
$$P\bigl(g(y),\, y\bigr) = 0$$
identically on the domain of $g$.

**Constructing the annihilating polynomial for $g$.** Define a new polynomial $Q \in \mathbb{R}[s,t]$ by swapping the two variables of $P$:
$$Q(s, t) := P(t, s).$$
Since $P$ is a polynomial in two variables, $Q$ is also a polynomial in two variables (and $Q$ is nonzero since $P$ is nonzero). Evaluating $Q$ at $(s,t) = (y, g(y))$:
$$Q\bigl(y,\, g(y)\bigr) = P\bigl(g(y),\, y\bigr) = 0.$$

Thus $Q(y, g(y)) = 0$ identically on the domain of $g$, with $Q \in \mathbb{R}[s,t]$ a nonzero polynomial. By definition, $g$ is algebraic.

**Remark on the domain.** The identity $P(x, f(x)) = 0$ holds on the open set where $f$ is defined. The inverse $g$ maps its domain into the domain of $f$, so the substitution $x = g(y)$ is valid for every $y$ in the domain of $g$, and the resulting identity $Q(y, g(y)) = 0$ holds on that entire (open) domain.

### PROOF COMPLETE
